[
    {
        "content": "<p>What is the best (in terms of underlying API development) way to state \"the k-th homology group of the n-sphere is <code>Int</code> if <code>k = 0,n</code> and trivial otherwise\", <strong>with the additional contraint</strong> that the n-sphere must be defined as <code>Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace (Fin (n+1)) Real) 1</code>?</p>",
        "id": 597394336,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Tan",
        "timestamp": 1779623681
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>More generally, what is the best way to link a geometric description of a topological space (sphere, torus, Klein bottle, etc.) with a purely algebraic (simplicial set or similar) definition?</p>",
        "id": 597394548,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Tan",
        "timestamp": 1779623886
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>With group cohomology we're using <code>\\iso 0</code> to say \"is zero\" (this is in a category-theoretic framework).</p>",
        "id": 597396119,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1779625305
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If <code>K</code> is a homological complex, the best way to say that it has zero homology in degree <code>n</code> is the predicate <code>K.ExactAt n</code> (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=HomologicalComplex.ExactAt#doc\">docs#HomologicalComplex.ExactAt</a>). If you need a statement in terms of the homology (rather than the complex), the other good formulation is <code>Limits.IsZero (K.homology 37)</code>. (It is better not to use <code> K.homology 37 ≅ 0</code>.)</p>",
        "id": 597409506,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1779637960
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree with the answers above. But maybe the more interesting part of <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"598052\">Jeremy Tan</span>'s question is how to express <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_k(\\mathbb{S}^n) \\cong \\Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 0,n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>?</p>\n<p>Because we have the <code>IsZero</code> predicate, but what about nonzero?</p>\n<p>Do we just use plain old isomorphisms? Do we say it's free of rank one?</p>",
        "id": 597733434,
        "sender_full_name": "Johan Commelin",
        "timestamp": 1779771419
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sometimes you have a preferred generator or basis, like the fundamental class for top homology of connected manifolds.</p>",
        "id": 597735518,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1779772562
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For <code>k = 0</code>, and <code>X</code> path connected, we already have the fact that the augmentation <code>((singularHomologyFunctor C 0).obj R).obj X ⟶ R</code> is an isomorphism <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37630\">#37630</a>. For <code>k = n</code> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X := \\mathbf{S}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, I think the best is to define a specific isomorphism (for which we may have to be careful with signs).</p>",
        "id": 597735672,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1779772640
    }
]